# React Native + Expo

Assume Expo SDK 52+, New Architecture (Fabric + TurboModules) enabled, Hermes, and expo-router.

## Two Threads, One Rule

The JS thread runs your logic; the UI thread paints frames. Anything that blocks JS for more than ~16ms drops a frame. Users do not read your profiler — they feel the stutter.

- Animations run on the UI thread via **Reanimated worklets**. The `Animated` API from core drives most animations through JS — avoid it for anything gesture-driven.
- `useAnimatedStyle`, `useSharedValue`, `withSpring` — a shared value mutation never triggers a React render, which is the point.
- Gestures via `react-native-gesture-handler`, not `PanResponder`.
- Heavy computation goes into `InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions` or off-thread entirely.

## Lists

`FlatList` degrades past a few hundred rows. Use **FlashList** and give it a real `estimatedItemSize` — a wrong estimate is worse than none.

- Row components are `memo`'d with a stable `keyExtractor`.
- Never an inline arrow in `renderItem` — it invalidates memoization on every parent render.
- Fixed row heights wherever the design allows; dynamic measurement is the expensive path.

## Navigation

expo-router, file-based, typed routes on.

- `_layout.tsx` per segment; `(tabs)`, `(auth)`, `(modal)` groups for structure without URL noise.
- `<Redirect>` for auth gating, not an imperative `router.replace` inside an effect — the effect version flashes protected content first.
- Deep links declared in `app.json` and tested on a real device before shipping.

## Platform Differences Are Real

- `Platform.select` for values; separate `.ios.tsx` / `.android.tsx` files when the divergence is structural.
- Safe areas via `react-native-safe-area-context`. Never a hardcoded status-bar height.
- Android back button, iOS swipe-back, keyboard avoidance, and haptics all need explicit handling.
- Shadows: `shadowColor`/`shadowOffset` on iOS, `elevation` on Android. Neither is a substitute for the other.

## Offline Is the Default State

Mobile networks fail constantly. Design for it rather than treating it as an error case.

- Persisted query cache so the app opens with data.
- Mutations queue and replay; every write carries a client-generated idempotency key.
- Optimistic updates with a real rollback path.
- Reachability changes are a UI state, not a toast.

## Native Modules

Prefer an Expo package. If you must write native code, use **Expo Modules API** (Swift/Kotlin) with a config plugin — not manual `ios/` and `android/` edits, which a prebuild will overwrite.

## Performance Discipline

- Never report a perf number you did not measure. "Feels faster" is not a metric — use the profiler and report p95 frame time and dropped-frame counts.
- Measure on a **low-end Android device**, not a simulator on an M-series Mac.
- Track bundle size per release; check for accidental full-library imports (`lodash`, icon packs).

## NEVER

- `console.log` left in a shipped bundle — it crosses the bridge on every call.
- `require` of a remote image without dimensions.
- Inline styles recreated per render — hoist to `StyleSheet.create`.
- Secrets in `app.json`/`app.config.js` — anything bundled is extractable from the IPA/APK.
- `alert()` — it is not a React Native API.
